    "raw": "Target: jovlinger/bin/mvx.py (invoked as bin/binlinks/mvx).\n\nPROBLEM\n\nmvx defaults to backgrounding via a bare os.fork() in main() (mvx.py ~line\n365): the parent returns to the shell immediately and the orphaned child does\nthe entire move. Consequences:\n\n- The worker is not a shell job. The shell only ever knew the parent, so\n  `jobs` / `fg` can never reach the worker.\n- The child never properly detaches either: it inherits the tty and keeps\n  scribbling progress dots and log lines over the next prompt.\n- There is no way to check progress, wait for completion, learn the exit\n  status (partial -> 2), or abort cleanly.\n\nWHY \"RE-CONNECT TO FG\" REQUIRES AN ARCHITECTURE CHANGE (the constraint)\n\nUnix has no mechanism to adopt an existing orphan into a shell's job table or\nto hand it a new controlling terminal. Session and controlling-tty membership\nare fixed at fork/setsid time, and `fg` operates only on the shell's own\nchildren. Every tool that appears to reattach (tmux, dtach, abduco, docker\nattach) works the same way: it interposes a rendezvous point AT LAUNCH and\nlater attaches a fresh foreground client to it. (reptyr-style ptrace fd\nhijacking is Linux-only and blocked by SIP on macOS -- not an option.)\n\nTherefore \"fg\" must be architected as ATTACH, not ADOPT: split mvx into a\nproperly detached worker plus a thin foreground attach client.\n\nPROPOSED ARCHITECTURE (attach model)\n\n1. Per-run job rendezvous dir: ~/.mvx/jobs/<jobid>/ where jobid is e.g.\n   YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-<pid>. Contents:\n     pid          worker pid\n     log          combined worker stdout/stderr (append-only)\n     status.json  {state: running|done, moved, removed, clashed, partial,\n                   current_file, exit_code, updated_at}\n2. Default (bg) path: parent forks; child calls setsid() and redirects\n   stdout/stderr into the log (real daemonization -- this also fixes the\n   tty-scribbling bug); parent prints\n   \"mvx: job <jobid> started (mvx fg to attach)\" and exits 0. Worker updates\n   status.json periodically and writes the final exit_code at completion.\n3. New attach mode `mvx fg [<jobid>]` (default: most recent still-running\n   job): streams the log tail -f style to the terminal, waits for worker\n   exit, then exits with the worker's recorded exit_code. Ctrl-C detaches by\n   default (worker keeps running); an explicit kill flag/subcommand signals\n   the worker (SIGTERM) to abort.\n4. `-f` keeps meaning pure foreground: no fork, no job dir, output straight\n   to the tty -- unchanged behavior.\n5. Deferred until proven needed: `mvx jobs` listing; a ctl unix socket for\n   pause/finer control. v1 needs neither -- log tail + pidfile + SIGTERM\n   covers attach, wait, and abort.\n\nREJECTED ALTERNATIVES\n\n- Making the shell's own `fg` reach the worker: impossible per the\n  constraint above.\n- reptyr / ptrace reattach: Linux-only, blocked by macOS SIP.\n- Wrapping in tmux/dtach: external dependency, changes invocation UX; mvx\n  output (dots + one summary line) does not need a real pty.\n- Full pty server (dtach-style, worker on a pty owned by a tiny server):\n  byte-exact tty semantics but overkill for line/dot progress; reconsider\n  only if mvx ever grows interactive prompts."
  },
